WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.155
Compiler, library, and release hardening features help catch some mistakes and reduce risk, but they do not replace safe code.

2
00:00:08.155 --> 00:00:15.902
They are defense-in-depth controls that work best alongside safer APIs, clear ownership, testing, review, and release verification.

3
00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:19.164
Warnings and static diagnostics catch risky patterns early.

4
00:00:19.164 --> 00:00:24.873
Suspicious conversions, ignored results, uninitialized reads, deprecated functions, and size mismatches are useful signals.

5
00:00:24.873 --> 00:00:31.397
Teams should fix important warnings or document narrow exceptions rather than hiding large classes of diagnostics.

6
00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:38.329
Stack protection, object-size-aware checks, and fortified library behavior can add runtime checks for selected patterns.

7
00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:42.406
Coverage depends on compiler, optimization, platform, library, and code shape.

8
00:00:42.406 --> 00:00:46.892
These features should be enabled deliberately and verified in release builds.

9
00:00:46.892 --> 00:00:55.047
Hardened standard library modes and debug runtimes can expose invalid iterator use, bounds mistakes, and invalid assumptions during development or

10
00:00:55.047 --> 00:00:55.454
testing.

11
00:00:55.454 --> 00:01:01.163
Release settings may differ for performance or compatibility, but the choice should be documented.

12
00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:06.056
Toolchain flags vary across GCC, Clang, MSVC, operating systems, architectures, and versions.

13
00:01:06.056 --> 00:01:11.357
Define the security intent first, then map that intent to the selected toolchain.

14
00:01:11.357 --> 00:01:18.289
Do not assume that a flag copied from another project has the same effect in your build.

15
00:01:18.289 --> 00:01:21.958
Release artifacts should be verified against the hardening policy.

16
00:01:21.958 --> 00:01:27.667
Check build logs, compiler and linker commands, binary properties, dependency settings, and packaging steps.

17
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:32.560
A policy is only useful when the delivered artifact actually reflects it.
